Arguably, carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ is the most well-known GHG and thus receives the most attention in the fight against climate change. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was the first GHG that the public could understand, discuss, and realize as being an environmental problem to which humans have contributed postindustrialization (anthropogenic). It is no wonder that it is therefore the base unit of measure for the GHG warming effects known as the global warming potential (GWP); the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -eq is a unit of measure of impact or how much energy the emissions will absorb compared to that of an equivalent 1 ton of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas, typically discussed over 100 years.2 For example, methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ has a GWP of $25\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -eq and, therefore, has 25 times the environmental impact of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
$\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ measures at $298\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -eq for a lifetime over 100 years. It is shockingly high and incredibly detrimental for the environment. Fortunately, $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ only composes $6\%$ of all the GWP emitted according to the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA).3 It is true that $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is an integral part of the nitrogen cycle and thus produced naturally within the biosphere; however, currently ${\sim}43\%$ of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ present in the nitrogen cycle is caused by anthropogenic activities.4 This suggests that roughly half of the $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ present would ideally not be present. The nitrogen cycle and process are reviewed in Figure 1 with the locations at which $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ occurs highlighted with a green triangle.
The most abundant form of nitrogen present is atmospheric ${\bf N}_{2};$ however, for plants to absorb nitrogen it must be converted to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ through biological processes, where the form depends upon the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ present.4 The conversion process is performed by nitrifying and denitrifying bacteria and proteins on a large scale, and it is still being studied by scientists today. Per the EPA in the GHG emission and sink report from 1990 to 2019, $75\%$ of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ emissions in the United States were sourced from agricultural soil
