which uses fossil fuels for the hydrogen production as well as a highly exergonic process for the conversion described in the equation. The Haber−Bosch process makes 100 million tons of nitrogen-containing fertilizer annually to supply the world’s agricultural needs.4 Fertilizers are not intrinsically damaging; after all, they aid in food growth for life on Earth. So how does something benign become such an issue? The concern arises from what one might call the overapplication of synthetic and organic fertilizers. For example, if a nitrogen-rich fertilizer is used for urban soil management such as a golf course, but the grass only absorbs a fraction of what is applied, then the excess is released into the nitrogen cycle within the soil and water.2 Further examples of anthropogenically introduced $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sources include wastewater treatment (nitrification or denitrification) to reduce or oxidize urea, $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ or manure and waste runoff management; however, runoff and agricultural biomass contribute to $4\%$ of the overall $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ emissions in 2019.
Therefore, in attempts to mitigate the impact of anthropogenic contributions to the nitrogen cycle (N cycle), there exist such unique solutions as the “MooLoo”. This entertaining and surprising approach was in-latrine training of livestock to readily capture and treat excreta rather than it entering the environment, water table, and $\mathbf{N}$ cycle, thus resulting in a significant reduction of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ emissions.5 Anthropogenic $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ is not a simple issue and will require innumerable minds inventing and testing novel ideas and working together to address small portions of the larger problem. An overview of GHG types is shown in Figure 2a, and additional $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sources are pictured in Figure 2b.
A first step to address the monumental problem of climate change, specifically focusing on ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O},$ is a better or complete understanding of the N cycle. To this day, every step, and the impacts of small changes made therein, are not fully understood.4 For example, there exists a partial understanding of the biogeochemical activation of ${\bf N}_{2}$ that involves large-scale dynamic and hydrolysis, to name a few, and yet it is performed under benign, natural conditions. The complexity, current understanding, and gaps in knowledge of the N cycle are best described by Lehnert et al. in a viewpoint piece titled “Grand challenges in the nitrogen cycle”.4 That said, it is essential to develop a sensor technology that can real-time monitor $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ emissions from various sources to identify points of the cycle where $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is present. To meet such needs, the sensor needs to be sensitive (e.g., as low as 25 ppb for environmental site identification), portable, and low-cost.6 Several methods for detecting $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ are being used such as infrared laser spectroscopy using quantum cascade lasers (QCL), chromatography, and amperometry .7−10 In this review, various portable $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensors [i.e., microelectromechanical system (MEMS)-based nondispersive infrared (NDIR) adsorption, electrochemical, and chemiresistive] are evaluated and compared. Additionally, we have suggested different sensing materials [metal−organic frameworks (MOFs)] that might be able to further increase sensing performance and functionality in $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ detection.
## PORTABLE $\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ SENSORS
Nondispersive Infrared Adsorption Technology Based $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathsf{O}}$ Sensor. Laser-based instruments are most widely used to detect $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensitively [e.g., sensing $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ as a breath biomarker for asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) with a sensitivity of 0.8 ppb $\mathrm{Hz}^{-\hat{1}/2}~\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ .8 Current available sensors on the market for purchase are primarily based upon nondispersive infrared (NDIR) absorption technology as seen in Table 1. Table 1 is by no means an exhaustive list but is composed of the top results from an Internet search for $^{\ast}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas sensors”.
NDIR is a simple spectroscopic method where the gas analyte is held in a small chamber where an IR source is passed through it to an IR detector where the wavelength corresponds to an absorption band specific to the gas in the cell.11 $\mathrm{\dot{N}}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ specifically, has its highest IR active absorption wavelength at $4.5\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ , the lowest possible perturbation energy required to identify $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .11 This sensing method can now have infrared emitting diodes (IREDs) as the light source and can be placed on a microelectromechanical system (MEMS) for portability and ease.11 That said, current $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensors available for purchase are all based upon the same technology, which is not without its limits, such as the interference or overlap in absorption wavelengths by nitric oxide $(5.3~\mu\mathrm{m})$ and carbon monoxide $(4.3~\mu\mathrm{m})$ . Furthermore, to maintain stability and long-term battery life, NDIR devices are typically made of single-channel sensors that are subject to IR and temperature drift.12,13 These systems cannot typically operate above $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and require about $30\mathrm{~s~}$ to $2\ \mathrm{min}$ gas diffusion time with a response time on the same order. Due to the fundamental principles of this measurement technique, the devices require a long optical path, especially if measuring gas mixtures, that contains multiple components such as a radiation source, beam splitters, detectors, optics, and filters that increase device complexity and cost.12−15
Although these systems have become smaller and more portable, the combined cost of materials and manufacturing make the NDIR method a somewhat costly detection method. Moreover, these methods require chambers to allow the accumulation of the gases to be analyzed and expensive optical components that make the techniques unsuitable for in situ $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensing and a complete understanding of the nitrogen cycle.
Spectroscopy-Based ${\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}}_{2}{\ensuremath{\mathsf{O}}}$ Sensors. In addition to NDIR technology, there exist numerous spectroscopy techniques that have been well-studied and showed promising results regarding accuracy and in situ sensing. For example, quantum cascade lasers (QCLs) are optic systems that use a stacked, atomiclevel-thickness semiconductor laser source focused through multiple lenses, most often in the mid-infrared range, that replaced the traditional lead salt laser systems.23 These systems have been shown to be capable of detecting and distinguishing multiple analytes. Wright et al. published results where a QCL system was mounted in an airplane and capable of continuous single-wavelength scans and detection of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ down to ppb levels.23 While the single, continuous scan was quick, the system suffered a significant amount of noise from the plane, causing a low signal-to-noise ratio requiring substantial high-frequency manipulation and removal.23 Several years later, the authors reduced this noise by creating an inlet mounted within the plane roof, thus making it possible to detect atmospheric trace gases.24 QCL systems have also been studied for soil-atmosphere exchange; however, as accurate as these systems have been proven to be compared to methods such as traditional mass spectrometry, they continue to require vacuum pumps, optics alignment, large housings, and analyte accumulation chambers.25−28
To enhance the resolution of these systems, QCLs have been integrated with photoacoustic spectroscopy (PAS), tunable IR laser differential absorption spectroscopy (TILDAS), wavelength modulation spectroscopy (WMS), multipass cells, and quartz-enhanced. $6,27,27-33$ These additions all aided in portability through either being convenient and capable of continuous measurements or reducing the physical size of the transducer using the piezoelectric effect. That said, these enhanced systems are not without their problems such as sensitivity to vibrations or noise and humidity.
Several systems, such as QCL and spectrometers, have further demonstrated the capabilities of these techniques by studying isotopomers. $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ isotopomer measurements are
particularly useful for soil environment investigations as they would allow for microbial source and sink process identifications, thus filling in knowledge gaps within the N cycle. For example, Mohn et al. developed a technique to simultaneously detect three main stable $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ isotopic species using QCL absorption spectroscopy.34 The technique was found to be superior to the more traditional isotope ratio mass spectrometry (IRMS) method.34 In a later publication, Harris et al. provided a calibration workflow for scientists to help with trace gas compositions for $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ isotope laser spectrome35,36   
