Electrochemical ${\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}}_{2}{\ensuremath{\mathsf{O}}}$ Sensors. To address the concern of ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , several methods have been developed throughout history for $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ detection. The electrochemical reduction of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ on a Pt cathode was first shown in an electrochemical oxygenmonitoring cell containing $30\%$ KOH. While this cyclic voltammetry at a Pt electrode was not an analytical method targeting ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , it does identify sensor-inhibiting species as well as $\mathrm{N}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{O}_{\boldsymbol{y}_{-}}$ species and their relative cyclic voltammetry features.38 It was also insightfully stated by the authors that “nitrous oxide...may be involved in the chemistry of air pollution”.38 From there, the analytical methods for detecting and quantifying $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ mainly relied upon electrochemical methods, where it was often difficult to deconvolute the contributions from $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ versus other gases such as dissolved oxygen.39−46 Table 2 compares $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensors based on electrochemical principles.
Direct Electrochemical Detection. $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ always exists in a mixture with other gases, commonly $\mathrm{O}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and the reduction potentials of the interfering gases (except $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{\cdot\dagger}$ ) are less negative than that of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ . The measured current is consequently the total current from the reduction of all gases in the mixture. Therefore, different approaches have been introduced to the configuration of the amperometric sensors to eliminate the interferents. The first amperometric microsensor, based on analyte interference work established by Albery et al. in 1979,40 was presented by Revsbech et al. in 1988 to simultaneously detect $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ .42 The authors built two different cathodesgold and silverfor separate reductions of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , respectively, in a common casing. After diffusing through the silicone rubber membrane at the electrode tip, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was reduced at the porous Au cathode whose potential was controlled to remove $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ but was not too negative to reduce $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ . Further negative potential was applied to the silver electrode to reduce ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , and the measured current was correlated with the concentration. An aqueous electrolyte was employed for this sensor such that a $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ change was observed due to the generation of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions from the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction.
To further mitigate the effects of gaseous interferents, Andersen et al. introduced the concept of a two-compartment sensor in which two different aqueous electrolytes were used for the reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ as shown in Figure 3a.48 An alkaline ascorbate solution, an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenger, served as the electrolyte for the oxygen guard compartment and thereby eliminated the need for a gold electrode for the reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . A silver cathode, $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ anode, and the electrolyte of KCl and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ were the components of the $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -sensing compartment. Two compartments were separated by the iontight silicon membrane. Moreover, the study suggested that the concentration of ascorbate solution and the distance between the components played important roles in the sensitivity of the sensor. High concentrations of ascorbate were favorable for the elimination of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ but slowed the diffusion of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and consequently reduced the response. In the same manner, the sensitivity of the sensor would be enhanced if the membranes were thinner and distance between them was shorter to promote the diffusion of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Besides $\mathrm{O}_{2},$ another interferent $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ was trapped in the alkaline ascorbate solution, albeit incompletely, and thereby diffused to the sensing compartment. Under polarization, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ caused a $\mathsf{p H}$ reduction, promoting the hydrogen evolution reaction. Lowering the applied potential lessened the interference of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the range of $0.1{-}0.2\ \mu\mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ but also resulted in the signal drifting and was not recommended for long-term monitoring.
With the possibility of water influx from the alkaline ascorbate solution causing an increase in the baseline of sensor response, organic solvents that could serve as the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenger were in demand. Also, an organic $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenger can possibly be used in a single-compartment sensor, which reduces the diffusion pathway simultaneously and corresponds to better performance of the sensor. After testing different phosphine derivatives, Sveegaard et al. reported diphenylphosphine in propylene carbonate as a good electrolyte for the compartment of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction.49 The two-compartment sensor using this electrolyte showed two times higher sensitivity and shorter response times in comparison to that using an alkaline ascorbate solution. The single-compartment sensors using diphenylphosphine were also successfully fabricated without sensitivity toward $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The developed sensors were capable of detecting $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as low as $20~\mathrm{nM}$ . The typical issue with analyte quantification at the low concentration range is the low signalto-noise ratio. Damgaard et al. came up with a clever method to resolve this problem.50 Instead of only diphenylphosphine for the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ removal in the front compartment, they integrated a switchable indium cathode that had the same material as the sensing cathode in the second compartment. The front cathode was periodically switched on to reduce the influx of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and only allow a small fraction of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to diffuse to the sensing cathode. The concentration of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ was correlated to the current difference between the on and off modes in the same fashion as the other amperometric $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas sensors. The authors reported that the newly fabricated sensors could achieve a sensitivity of $2{\mathrm{~nM~N}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ .
In addition to eliminating the interferents, attempts to deconvolute the reduction current of each gas from the total measured limiting current were conducted by Hahn’s research group, as mentioned in the introductory section.47,55,56 With the utilization of only one gold microdisc cathode, a silver anode, and an aprotic solvent (dimethyl sulfoxide), the authors first investigated the binary gas mixture of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathrm{~O}_{2},$ whose reduction potentials were well-separated. Furthermore, the two limiting currents were additive, so the concentration of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ could be determined by subtracting the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration. When $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was added to the mixture as an interferent, the scenario became more complex.55 The possibility of analytical determination of individual gases depends on their relative concentrations. When the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was low relative to that of ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ did not show the crossinterference with $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . However, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction started to show a different behavior at a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- ${\bf\cdot N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ volume ratio of ${>}1$ , which might be problematic for the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ detection at low concentrations. With the same electrochemical cell, the authors reported the solution for the severe $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cross-interference by substituting the long-time interval linear voltammetry with potential step chronoamperometry at sufficiently short time intervals.56 The short duration of applied potential resulted in a lower concentration of side-reaction products and thereby alleviated the cross-interferent reactions. Furthermore, the diffusion-layer thickness became smaller at a shorter applied potential interval and stayed within the electrolyte layer between the electrode surface and the polytetrafluorethylene membrane.
The electrodes of amperometric $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensors that were discussed were miniaturized in the microscale to facilitate the performance of the sensor. As generally accepted in all studies, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ is the main product of the $\mathrm{\bfN}_{2}\mathrm{\bfO}$ reduction reaction, which results in bubble formation on the cathode surface as the surface area is large enough to accommodate a buildup of ${\bf N}_{2}$ .48 Moreover, less disturbance from $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ buildup occurs in smaller sensors, which can then contribute to shorter diffusion pathways for $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .42,48 Instead of the three-electrode configuration, the microsensors employ only two due to the small current and low ohmic drop. The materials of the sensing cathode, mainly silver and indium, were selected due to their catalytic activity toward $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reduction. Other materials have also shown promising performance for integrating into $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensors, such as palladium, tetraaminophthalocyanato cobalt(II), and metal porphyrins.44,45,57
Indirect Electrochemical Detection. Direct detection of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas utilizes $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ gas as the reactant for the reduction reaction in eqs 2 and 3. On the other hand, the concentration of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas is analyzed indirectly by the reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2},$ which is the product of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ decomposition at high temperature, as shown in eqs 4 and 5. Because the indirect detection of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ requires the decomposition of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at high temperatures $(500-700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , a solid-state electrolyte is used for the amperometric sensor, specifically yttria-stabilized zirconia as the oxygen conductor, as seen in Figure 3b.43,51 In both cases, platinum plates serving as cathode and anode were positioned on two sides of the solid electrolyte and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was reduced at the cathode to form oxygen ions that diffused through the electrolyte. A higher applied potential resulted in more reduced oxygen and higher current density until the limiting current was reached. Because the limiting current is dependent on the transport limitation of the reactant, temperature is an important factor affecting the diffusion coefficient and the accuracy of the measurement. By assuming the complete decomposition of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and thermal stability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , the composition of the original gas mixture can be determined using the concentration of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ after thermal decomposition. Therefore, the correlation between $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations and the limiting current of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction can be utilized to calculate the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ concentration.
Different from the amperometric sensors that rely on the reduction of reactants, mixed-potential-based sensors are based on the reduction and oxidation of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{~O}_{2},$ respectively, as shown in eqs $_{3-4}$ and 2.52 The detection principle is the correlation of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ concentration and the value of the mixed potential at which the reduction and oxidation rates are equal. In their work, Kanazawa et al. employed the solid electrolyte (e.g., yttria-stabilized zirconia tube), 0.5 wt $9\%$ $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as the working electrode, and Pt as the reference or counter electrode. The addition of $S_{\mathrm{{m}_{2}\mathrm{{O}_{3}}}}$ was to suppress the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ for the decomposition of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ into $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at $450\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and thus they were able to maintain the composition of the gas mixture for the reduction and oxidation reactions.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\displaystyle\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{N}_{2}+\mathrm{O}^{2-}}}}\\ {{\mathrm{O}^{2-}\rightarrow\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}}}\end{array}
$$
## CHEMIRESISTIVE $\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ SENSORS
Chemiresistive gas sensors have become the most studied and commercially available type of gas sensor because of their costeffectiveness, ease of fabrication and integration with sensing materials, and simple signal-acquisition process.59 Most of the commercially available chemiresistive gas sensors are based on metal oxides (MOSs) due to an elegant simplicity in that the sensing mechanism is better understood than other complex materials as well as higher sensitivity and response/recovery times.60 Upon exposure to analytes, MOSs show a resistance change by oxidative interactions with the negatively charged adsorbed oxygen, as pictured in Figure 4 on the left side of either (a) or (b) where n-type and p-type MOSs, respectively, are distinguished.
The polarity, or shape, of response depends upon the type of material, in this case, whether they are n- or p-type, meaning that the charge carrier in each type is either electrons or holes, respectively. On the basis of the most widely accepted MOS gas-sensing mechanism, which is the oxygen adsorption and desorption model, when $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ molecules (or an oxidative environment) are adsorbed onto the material surface, they extract electrons from the conduction band, creating an electron-depletion zone.60−62 The electron-depletion region forms due to the consumption of electrons that are reacting with oxygen molecules; therefore, when $\mathfrak{n}$ -type sensing materials are exposed to reducing analytes, electrons are released back to the conduction band, which leads to a decrease in resistance (as illustrated in Figure 4a). Conversely, for a p-type MOS, oxygen adsorption results in a holeaccumulation region in the sensing material where, upon exposure to reducing analytes, the resistance will increase, and vice versa in an oxidative atmosphere.
As evident by the sensing mechanism of the two types of MOSs, it is beneficial, and safe, to segregate gas analytes into either oxidative or reductive categories. From literature precedence, it is apparent that $\mathfrak{n}$ -type MOSs are studied more often, most likely due to their stronger responses when compared to p-type.62 Each material type has its benefits and drawbacks, and therefore, the best approach would most likely be a combinatorial approach, especially when considering the complexity of our environment, such as $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunctions or multilayered materials.63 That said, $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ is an oxidizing gas, and the oxidizing reaction between metal oxide and $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is shown in the following reaction path:64
$$
\mathrm N_{2}\mathrm O(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm e^{-}\to\mathrm N_{2}\mathrm O^{-}(\mathrm{ads})
$$
$$
\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{-}(\mathrm{ads})\rightarrow\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{(gas)+O}^{-}(\mathrm{ads})
$$
In 1999, Kanazawa and co-workers first acknowledged that popular n-type metal oxides such as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2},\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},\mathrm{ZnO},$ and ${\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ were sensitive to ${\bf N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , but insufficiently so, and by doping $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with 0.5 wt $\%\mathrm{SrO}$ , Kanazawa could consistently detect 10−300 ppm of N2O in air between 300 and 600 °C.65 Arguably, the largest drawback to MOSs is that they require high temperatures to operate because the adsorbed oxygen requires temperatures from 100 to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to become an oxygen anion, $0^{-}$ , based on the most widely accepted sensingmechanism model, which can then migrate and remove electrons that cause the cascading conductivity response discussed earlier.60−62
Wong et al. proposed the photoassisted transformation of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ to $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ on the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ surface, which indicated that photoexcitation would not only achieve low operating tmeemcphearnaistumreas fwoerl saesnpsirnovg ibnug alhsiog crhsaengsei thre soensei.n6g6 Mitigation of these higher temperatures could allow for complex combinations of materials such as metal−organic frameworks or organic-coated MOSs; however, prior to optimization, materials capable of sensitively and selectively detecting $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ should be explored.
For a more comprehensive evaluation, we have separated simple MOSs and complex MOSs, which refers to pristine/ doped MOSs and complex metal oxide systems with crystal structures such as spinels, ferrites, and perovskites, respectively. As both the compositional and crystal structures change from simple to complex MOSs, the gas-sensing performance and capabilities differ quite significantly and therefore should be assessed separately.
The most comprehensive $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOS sensor is reported by Kanazawa, whereupon the author was able to increase the sensor’s sensitivity to $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in air by 0.5 wt $\%$ doping of $\mathrm{\mathop{SrO}-}$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ which laid the groundwork for subsequent $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -derived MOS chemiresistive sensors.67 The library of works for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was expanded by doping $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with different oxides such as $S_{\mathrm{{m}_{2}O_{3},}}$ CaO, or $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ to name a few.52 At elevated temperatures of $475^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ only $S_{\mathrm{{m}_{2}\mathrm{{O}_{3}}}}$ showed an appreciable response to $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .52 Furthermore, to confirm and understand the success of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to other single metal oxides, Kanazawa tested 23 additional single, pristine metal oxides, and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ continued to have a superior sensitivity to $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ at $300~\mathrm{ppm}$ with almost double the sensing capability.67 Although $S\mathrm{m}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{-}$ or $\mathrm{\Delta}S_{\mathrm{r}}O$ -doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ displayed an appreciable increase in sensitivity to ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O},$ , those values were still not sufficient for monitoring $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in practical applications, and further detailed studies should be performed to establish crystallinity, material composition, and system optimization. Upon doping with noble metals, transition metal oxides, rare-earth metal oxides, and others, 0.5 wt $\%\ \mathrm{SrO}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ continued to have the highest sensitivity.67 It is speculated that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has the best sensitivity in that it had the ideal balance between diffusivity and reactivity, as pictured in Figure 5c, where if there exists insufficient diffusivity, $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reactivity and decomposition occur upon the surface of the material, never resulting in detection.67 Specifically, if $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules are unable to diffuse, they are typically consumed and decompose upon the surface, similar to the behavior of a catalyst such as those seen in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ -doped $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ .67 Furthermore, slow diffusion prevents the formation and movement of an $\mathrm{O}^{-}$ adsorbed species, which is required in the MOS sensing mechanism, as seen in Figure 4a and b.67 Table 3 summarizes the $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensing performance based on MOSs.
Most notably, from Table 3, Au-doped $S_{\mathrm{nO}_{2}},$ $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ and ${\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ have an increased sensitivity greater than that first established with $\mathrm{SrO}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . By loading $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with Au, grown by ArF excimer laser-induced deposition (MOCVD), Lančok et al. were able to increase the sensing performance while simultaneously lowering the operating temperature to 210 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .69 Although the cause of enhancement has not been reported, the authors observed that the most sensitive materials were those synthesized at room temperature, making them amorphous and having a higher atomic carbon content.69 By solvothermally growing nanowires ${\sim}20\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, Rout et al. exhibited a higher sensing performance at low gas concentrations, thus highlighting the importance of morphology in gas sensing. In this example, the nanowire had a larger surface area-to-volume ratio, increasing the sensing active sites; however, further materials characterization and sensor fabrication investigations should be performed.68 Additionally, the crystallinity and grain size of MOS sensing materials affect the sensing performance, which could be another path to optimize N2O-sensing behaviors.71
Beyond binary oxides or doped binary oxides, there exist complex metal oxide systems with crystal structures such as spinels, ferrites, and perovskites. While these materials have not been extensively studied, they have shown some efficiency and selectivity for specific gases as well as being versatile in applications such as ferrofluids.70,72,73 For example, the nanocrystalline $\mathrm{Mg}_{0.5}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was found to have a sensitivity of $5.0625\times10^{-4}$ to $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ at $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .70 This value, however, was in ${\sim}1600~\mathrm{ppm}$ gas concentration, and the material displayed higher sensitivity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CO.70 Unfortunately, beyond this magnesium zinc ferrite, there is little to no literature available regarding complex MOSs for $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ gas sensing. There does exist such materials as $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanocubes, ${\mathrm{Ti}}_{3}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{T}}_{x}$ MXene composites for methanol sensing, graphene $\mathbf{-CeO}_{2}$ for carbon monoxide, multiwalled carbon nanotubes/ $\mathrm{wo}_{3}$ for ${\mathrm{~\thinspaceSO}}_{2},$ photoactivated MOS for $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ copper ferrite for ethanol, $\mathrm{ZnSnO}_{3}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ and p-type $\mathrm{Na}_{0.44}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ nanoribbons for ethanol, to name a few .74−80 N2O decomposition over perovskite-types, ceria-based binary oxides, spinels, metal oxides, and hexaluminates is reviewed by Konsolakis, but as described earlier, for ideal sensing mechanisms there needs to be a balance between decomposition and diffusion of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ into the sensing material.81 Njoroge et al. provide a thorough review of spinel ferrites as gas sensors; however, there is no emphasis or attention placed on $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensitivity.82
In addition to MOS-based sensors, it has also been shown that organic semiconductors can detect $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at close to room temperature; however, their lack of sensitivity has been attributed to grain boundaries in the past.31 Fukuda et al. demonstrated that an organic film made of poly-3-alkylthiophene, poly-3-hexylthiophene organic thin-film transistor (P3HT OTFT), could detect $1000~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by monitoring the hole density in the P3HT film, as seen in the experimental schematic in Figure 5b.28,31
## OTHER POSSIBLE SENSING MATERIALS
We have presented all the published work to date for $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas sensors based on experimental data and, as a result, focused on simulation work of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas sensing accomplished by various groups. In contrast to the Portable $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ Sensors section, where most or all of the $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas sensors were made from MOS-based sensing materials, transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), carbanion materials, and other 2D structures were utilized for $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ -sensing simulation via calculating the total energies as well as equilibrium structures upon exposure to carrier gas and analytes, so-called first principle density functional theory (DFT).83 Such methods allow us to find potential sensing materials for specific analytes prior to performing experiments.
For example, Wanbayor et al. investigated $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption on the carbon monoxide (CO) preadsorbed anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (1 0 1) surface via first principle calculations.83 Two types of adsorption configurations were found in this work for neutral surface adsorption of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , including two types of physisorption $\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=-0.17\right.$ and $-0.13\ \mathrm{eV},$ and one type of chemisorption $'\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}~=~-2.21~\mathrm{eV})$ ). For $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption on the negative surface of the anatase ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ one type of physisorption $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}^{\mathrm{~~}}=-0.01~\mathrm{eV})$ ) and two types of chemisorption $\'\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=-0.74$ and $-2.24~\mathrm{eV},$ ) were obtained. However, there is no data for the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ adsorption without CO preadsorption.
Mishra and Pandey demonstrated a 2D $\mathbf{MoSe}_{2}$ material for potential $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensing, similar to MOSs.84 On the basis of their results, adsorption energies of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ increased with the decrease in the adsorbent distance from 2 to $1\mathrm{~\AA~}$ as well as a significantly improved charge transfer. Meanwhile, the band gap of 2D $\mathbf{MoSe}_{2}$ shifted from 1.45 to $1.39~\mathrm{eV}$ for pristine and postadsorption, respectively. Such material properties variation would contribute to the signal change for gas sensing.
In addition to MOS and TMD sensing materials, carbanion materials such as graphene and Al-doped graphene have been reported by Rad as superior $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}$ adsorbents.85 In another example, Rad demonstrated the absorbability of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ on $\mathrm{Pt}$ -decorated graphene, which enhanced the potential of graphene as an adsorber of $\bar{\bf N}_{2}\bar{\bf O}$ .86 Other than graphene, Liu et al. studied the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ adsorption onto the surface of metal (Mg, Cu, or $\mathrm{Ag}{\dot{}}$ ) decorated graphene oxide (GO), which implied the possibility of $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{u}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{G}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}$ for $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensing.87 Delving further into carbanion materials, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) were investigated for the capability of Pd-doped single-wall carbon nanotube (Pd-SWCNT) adsorption of nitrous oxide by Yoosefian.88 Notably, the $\mathrm{Pd-C}$ bond length would increase due to the strong interaction between Pd and $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ as well as slightly increasing the band gap energy of the Pd-SWCNT, whereas no change was observed for the pristine SWNCT. By showing that $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules are capable of being chemisorbed onto Pd-SWCNT with large adsorption energies and remarkable charge transfer, $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ detection based on Pd-SWCNT, or Pd-doped sensing material development, is expected. Lastly, the sensing performance of boron nitride (BN) and Al-BN nanosheets toward $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was calculated and reported by Samadizadeh et al.89 As a result of the highly geometric, hexagonal structures and electronic properties found in the calculation, it is suggested that AlBN would behave with a higher reactivity toward $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ via van der Waals forces than pristine BN nanosheets, thus making it a potential candidate for detecting and monitoring $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
A combination of transition metal and organic-carbon materials is realized within the ubiquitous MOF system. A metal−organic framework contains organic linkers varying in complexity connected to metal centers, thus creating a cage-like motif, with one-, two-, or three-dimensional structures possible, as seen from left to right in Figure 6.
MOFs tend to have extremely high surface areas and porosities with a wide variety of compositions due to the possible structural combinations from the organic and inorganic components. Additionally, MOFs show promise due to their postsynthetic tunability. For example, a zinc-containing MOF could be synthesized in a specific morphology not found in direct $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ synthesis, but upon high temperature heating, it converts to $\mathrm{znO}$ but retains the rhombohedral MOF morphology.
There exist over 20 000 different MOFs; however, there seem to be very few being used or integrated into gas sensors.90 Conversely, it is not uncommon to see MOFs used as catalysts, filters, energy or chemical storage, or for environmental monitoring applications within aqueous environments.90−92 An excellent review article by Koo et al. discusses and summarizes the gas analytes and MOFs as both sensors and selective membranes for gas sensing.93 Unfortunately, even with a huge library of possible compositions and structures, no MOF-based sensor for $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ currently exists. Most MOF and MOF derivative sensors have the highest sensitivity for volatile organic compounds such as formaldehyde, benzene, ethanol, acetone, etc.94
Because MOSs are so strongly dependent upon surface interactions of the target gas molecules and adsorbed oxygen species and composite gas sensors are ruled by the majority phase, it is our suggestion that MOS−MOF composites or MOF additives may aid in enhancing $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -sensing capabilities. For example, the porosity of MOFs could be leveraged to create permselective membranes that only allow penetration of certain target gases, thus eliminating the difficulty faced when additional gases interfere with $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ detection. This method was achieved by Yao et al. when they prepared $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO}}_{\mathcal{Q}}$ ZIF-CoZn core−sheath nanowire arrays that had increased sensitivity toward acetone.95 Another example is that by Koo et al., where the authors coated Pd nanowires with ZIF-8 for increased response and recovery times of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas sensing.96 Both systems used zeolitic imidazole framework 8 (ZIF-8), which is a subset of MOFs, that resembles the $_{\mathrm{O-Si-O}}$ bond in zeolites but contains multiple types of inorganic metals. ZIF-8 is a hydrophobic material that is relatively easy to synthesize, thus making it an ideal sort of membrane to prevent moisture interference. Unfortunately, both systems suffer from a lack of uniform distribution and have poor long-term stability.93 Many remarkable achievements have been made in the studies of MOFs; however, they do continue to suffer long-term and high-temperature stability issues. Furthermore, while many MOF systems have been studied as gas sensors, there are still many analytes to which these systems could be applied, such as $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
## CONCLUSION AND OUTLOOK
It was only recently that $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ started receiving more scientific attention despite the potential negative impacts from anthropogenically created $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . As seen in Figure 7a, scientific publications featuring $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensors have increased in the past 25 years yet are 2 orders of magnitude smaller than those of other analyte sensors such as carbon monoxide (CO) and nitric oxide $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)$ . A further breakdown of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ publications in recent years exhibits a high focus on optical and carbon systems, as shown in Figure $76$ . Work on understanding the $\mathbf{N}$ cycle is promising, but a detailed understanding has not yet been achieved due to its complexity and fluctuating nature. To better identify and gauge the location of $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in the N cycle, we propose a shift in scientific focus to developing more selective and sensitive $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ gas sensors for real-time monitoring. Such a device would be capable of detecting and monitoring $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in multiple types of environments (land, water, and air), yet it would be unobtrusive to nature and farming. An ideal solution may be a combination of MOS and electrochemical sensors, depending on the media, that can transfer data in situ, as conceptually shown in Figure 8 and typically referred to as “smart farming”. To that end, we have summarized MOS chemiresistive and electrochemical sensors for $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensing and hope to have convinced the reader of the need for further exploration into $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gas-sensing capabilities and perhaps even encouraged readers and scientists to use MOSs as a materials platform for potential composite $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ gas sensors.
